menu "tinylink core"

    config TINYLINK_APP_TASK_WDT
        bool "Subscribe application tasks to the task watchdog"
        default y
        help
            wg_rx, wg_tx, telemetry, the DERP supervisor and the map
            long-poll subscribe to the ESP task WDT and feed it once per
            loop iteration (blocking TLS reads feed through the tls_io
            poll hook). With CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC=y a task that
            stops iterating for CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S reboots
            the device into the known-good boot path. Disable for bench
            debugging with a debugger attached (breakpoints would trip
            it).

    config TINYLINK_CONTROL_PLAIN_HTTP
        bool "ts2021 over plain HTTP :80 first, TLS :443 as the fallback"
        default y
        help
            Open the control connection (POST /ts2021, Noise IK) over
            plain TCP port 80 before TLS port 443, as the upstream client
            does (control/controlhttp): the Noise IK handshake
            authenticates the server by its pinned public key, so TLS
            only adds a second cipher and ~2 s of handshake (chain
            verify + ECDHE) to the boot and every reconnect. Only when
            the control key was pinned before the boot (NVS pin or
            CONFIG_TINYLINK_CONTROL_PUB_FALLBACK_HEX — a first-boot TOFU
            fetch keeps that boot on TLS) and with the default control
            port 443. Any :80 failure (refused, no answer within 3 s, a
            redirect from a reverse proxy, a failed Noise handshake)
            falls back to :443 in the same connect, and a :80 failure
            that :443 then gets past keeps the rest of the boot on :443.
            Disable for networks that silently drop port 80: they pay a
            3-s connect timeout once per boot.

    config TINYLINK_CONTROL_PUSH_PIPELINE
        bool "Endpoint push as a second HTTP/2 stream beside the map stream"
        default n
        help
            Round 1.3.7 (B1): the boot endpoint push (a lite MapRequest)
            goes in the same burst as the streaming MapRequest, one round
            trip less (~0.2 s to the netmap). Off by default: against
            tailscale.com the map stream of every such connection was
            later ended cleanly by the server (2 of 2 on the sensor, at
            229 s and 645 s; with the push first that happened once in
            ~30 boot connections of 1.3.5–1.3.6, plus twice mid-soak on
            1.2.0) and the next poll then got no answer for 120 s, the
            one after it its netmap only 26–55 s late (59–112 s after
            the earlier clean ends). Off, the push goes first and the
            stream after it on the same connection, as up to 1.3.6.

    config TINYLINK_WARM_START
        bool "WireGuard up from the previous boot's netmap (warm start)"
        default y
        help
            Round 1.3.9. Once this boot's register has succeeded without
            the auth key (control answered for the node it knows, with
            these keys), the WireGuard data plane comes up from a record
            of the previous boot's netmap — our tailnet address and the
            target peer's NodeID, NodeKey, DiscoKey, endpoints and
            tailnet addresses (NVS tl_state "warm_rec", src/tl_warm.h) —
            instead of after this boot's netmap, so the handshake runs
            while the endpoint push and the map stream do (on the 1.3.8
            sensor the netmap was complete 1.4–5.5 s after the
            register). The first netmap confirms it, updates the peer's
            keys, endpoints or tailnet address in place, or — when the
            session cannot follow: another target peer, another self
            address, no peers, this node expired or deleted — erases the
            record and restarts into a cold boot (the next boot is cold
            even if the erase fails). Not used: a record no netmap stood
            behind for 30 days (it is refreshed daily while current), and
            after a reset (panic, WDT, brown-out) during a warm start
            before it settled (its netmap applied and the next boot's
            record stored, within about a minute of the netmap) or after
            a warm start that hung — the record is erased; after two
            such failures the warm start stays off until a power cycle.

            Trade-off: until that netmap arrives the device trusts the
            previous boot's peer binding — this NodeKey and this DiscoKey
            are the peer at that tailnet address — which an authenticated
            netmap gave it, possibly long ago. A peer key rotated or
            revoked since then (the peer removed, its key leaked) is
            still dialled: whoever holds the old NodeKey's private key
            can answer the handshake (via DERP, which routes by NodeKey)
            and receive telemetry and /stats replies; whoever holds the
            old DiscoKey's can get its DISCO pings answered, have the
            endpoints of its CallMeMaybe punched and move the WireGuard
            endpoint with a pong (the frames stay encrypted to the
            session). Until the netmap: normally seconds; at most
            TINYLINK_WARM_START_MAX_S plus up to a minute (the 60-s
            housekeeping checks it). Upstream tailscaled trusts its last
            netmap the same way within a run (through a control outage),
            as tinylink does after its first netmap. No packet filter is
            skipped: tinylink enforces none (docs/SECURITY-MODEL.md).
            Without a reachable control plane there is no warm start —
            the register comes first. n: the data plane waits for the
            netmap, as in 1.3.8.

    config TINYLINK_WARM_START_MAX_S
        int "Warm start: restart cold if no netmap confirms it within (s)"
        depends on TINYLINK_WARM_START
        default 600
        range 60 86400
        help
            How long a warm-started session may run on the previous
            boot's netmap alone, when control answered the register but
            no netmap followed (a map stream that stalls or is refused,
            an outage right after the register). Past it the record is
            erased and the device restarts; that cold boot waits for the
            netmap, as without the warm start. Checked by the telemetry
            task's 60-s housekeeping, so up to a minute late. 600 s is
            over three times the longest stretch without a netmap seen
            on the sensor (181 s after a clean stream end, 1.3.7, before
            1.3.8's first-response deadline).

    config TINYLINK_CRYPTO_IN_IRAM
        bool "Place the ChaCha20-Poly1305 hot path in IRAM"
        default y
        help
            Marks chacha20(), poly1305 blocks/update/finish and the AEAD
            glue IRAM_ATTR (4.3 KiB of the ~56 KiB free IRAM). Removes the
            flash-cache refill cost (~225 CPU cycles per 32-byte line)
            when TLS/WiFi code has evicted the crypto between packets.
            Measured 2026-09-04 with TINYLINK_BENCH_AEAD's cold variant
            on the ESP32-WROOM-32E: MTU packet 545 → 391 us (−28 %),
            64-byte packet 157 → 47 us (−70 %); hot-loop numbers do not
            move (385 us / 31.6 us). Off only if IRAM runs short.

    config TINYLINK_POWER_DIAG
        bool "Power diagnostic lines in the 60-s stack diag"
        default n
        depends on PM_ENABLE && FREERTOS_USE_TICKLESS_IDLE
        select PM_LIGHT_SLEEP_CALLBACKS
        select FREERTOS_GENERATE_RUN_TIME_STATS
        help
            Appends three "pwr diag:" lines to every 60-s stack diag:
            light-sleep residency, sleeps per second split by what
            ended them (an esp_timer alarm vs a FreeRTOS timeout),
            sleep-length bins, RTOS-tick drift against esp_timer, and
            run time per task (src/pwr_diag.h has the format). The
            first dump only arms it. Cost (round 1.3.7): +2.8 KB
            image, +72 B IRAM, +1.1 KB static DRAM; no extra wake, but
            the run-time stats add an esp_timer read to every context
            switch and the callbacks run in every idle-to-sleep
            transition, so it is for diagnostic images only
            (tools/power/sdkconfig.power-diag).

    config TINYLINK_TLS_RSA_FIRST
        bool "TLS: offer the ECDHE-RSA suites first (RSA certificate chain)"
        default y
        help
            Round 1.3.8, for the DERP and control (:443) connects; the
            first-boot /key fetch, whose TLS alone vouches for the
            control key it pins, keeps the full offer. The Tailscale
            servers serve an ECDSA chain to a ClientHello that lists an
            ECDHE-ECDSA suite and an RSA chain to one that does not: leaf RSA-2048 <- YR2 <- Root YR <- ISRG
            Root X1 instead of leaf P-256 <- YE2 <- Root YE <- ISRG Root
            X2 <- X1. Verifying the RSA chain and its ServerKeyExchange is
            four RSA public-key operations (exponent 65537, on the RSA
            accelerator) instead of three P-384 and one P-256 ECDSA
            verifies in software. Same bundle, same chain validation,
            same X25519 ECDHE and AES-128-GCM records; the server is
            authenticated by RSA-2048 (about 112-bit) instead of P-256
            (about 128-bit). A server that refuses the offer is dialled
            again at once with the full mbedTLS offer (not after a
            certificate for another name), and asked with it directly
            for 6 h (src/tls_offer.h).

    config TINYLINK_TLS_X25519_INTREE
        bool "TLS: X25519 ECDHE on the in-tree Curve25519"
        default y
        help
            Round 1.3.8. Both X25519 scalar multiplications of a full TLS
            handshake (the client's ephemeral key and the shared secret)
            run on the constant-time Curve25519 the WireGuard data plane
            uses (src/crypto/curve25519_fiat32.c) instead of mbedTLS's
            Montgomery ladder over its generic bignum. The link wraps
            mbedtls_ecp_mul_restartable (-Wl,--wrap) and diverts
            Curve25519 only; same argument checks and results as mbedTLS
            (src/tls_x25519.c). CONFIG_TINYLINK_BENCH_TLS cross-checks
            the two on the device.

    config TINYLINK_TLS_RESUME_RTC
        bool "TLS: keep the resumption session across warm resets (RTC memory)"
        depends on ESP_TLS_CLIENT_SESSION_TICKETS
        default y
        help
            Round 1.3.8. Every TLS connect that keeps a session (the DERP
            client, the control conn on :443) also writes it, serialized
            by mbedTLS, into one of two records in RTC slow memory (1.1
            KB). After a software, panic, interrupt-WDT or task-WDT
            reset — the ones that keep RTC memory and the RTC timer — the
            first connect to that server resumes (one round trip, no
            chain verify, no ECDHE) instead of a full handshake. Same
            bounds as the RAM copy (host:port, 24 h of master-secret age
            on the RTC timer, a failed connect clears it), plus a
            checksum against garbage and a refusal when the RTC timer
            reads earlier than the record's last write; any other boot,
            and an expired record, are scrubbed at the boot's first use.
            Never written to flash: src/tls_rtc.h has why.

    config TINYLINK_TLS_MPI_MUL_SW
        bool "TLS: bignum multiplication in software (RSA exponentiation stays on the accelerator)"
        depends on MBEDTLS_HARDWARE_MPI
        default y
        help
            Round 1.3.8. With CONFIG_MBEDTLS_HARDWARE_MPI alone every
            mbedtls_mpi_mul_mpi / mbedtls_mpi_mul_int goes to the RSA
            accelerator, operands padded to at least 512 bits, with a
            lock, a peripheral clock enable + reset and the operand
            copies per call. The worst case is on the RSA path: the R^2
            mod N of every RSA public key (a software long division)
            multiplies the 4096-bit modulus by one 32-bit limb per step,
            which the accelerator does as two padded 2048-bit
            multiplications. y undefines MBEDTLS_MPI_MUL_MPI_ALT
            (mbedtls_user_config.h): the multiplications run in mbedTLS's
            software bignum, while mbedtls_mpi_exp_mod (the RSA
            exponentiation itself) keeps the accelerator. QEMU
            instruction counts (the accelerator's own latency not
            modelled): RSA chain verify 9.7 M -> 6.2 M; an ECDSA P-384
            verify 38 M -> 47 M, the ECDSA fallback path's A/B is the
            on-chip CONFIG_TINYLINK_BENCH_TLS (tools/tls_bench/).

    config TINYLINK_TLS_ECP_WINDOW
        int "TLS: mbedTLS ECP window size (MBEDTLS_ECP_WINDOW_SIZE)"
        range 2 7
        default 4
        help
            Round 1.3.8 A/B knob, mbedTLS's default 4. The comb window
            of a scalar multiplication by a point other than the curve's
            generator: the u2*Q half of every ECDSA verify and the P-256
            / P-384 ECDHE. Larger windows take fewer point additions and
            a 2^(w-1)-point heap table. Measured (QEMU instruction
            counts, P-384 verify): w=2 +11 %, w=3 +3 %, w=5..7 -0.3 % for
            +3.5 KB of transient heap (P-256 does not go past 4). 4
            stays. The generator's fixed-point tables
            (CONFIG_MBEDTLS_ECP_FIXED_POINT_OPTIM) do not depend on it.

    config TINYLINK_BENCH_TLS
        bool "Run the TLS handshake crypto benchmark on boot"
        default n
        help
            Round 1.3.8. When y, bringup() calls tinylink_bench_tls()
            first thing, before NVS and Wi-Fi: the signature verifies of
            the two chains the Tailscale servers serve (ECDSA P-384 /
            P-256, RSA-2048 / RSA-4096, from the certificates themselves),
            the full chain verify of each through the certificate bundle,
            and the ECDHE (keygen + shared secret) for X25519 (in-tree
            and mbedTLS), P-256 and P-384, each timed on the chip with
            the image's mbedTLS configuration, plus heap and stack use.
            Build one image per configuration under test
            (tools/tls_bench/). Off by default; opt-in for perf work.

    config TINYLINK_BENCH_AEAD
        bool "Run ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD and X25519 micro-benchmarks on boot"
        default n
        help
            When y, bringup() calls tinylink_bench_aead() and
            tinylink_bench_x25519() once after tinylink_init() and
            before register. AEAD: RFC 8439 KAT (aligned and unaligned
            buffers), µs/call, MB/s and ns/byte for 64B and 1500B
            payloads, hot and cold cache, raw ChaCha20 per MTU. X25519:
            RFC 7748 KAT, µs per scalarmult, CCOUNT spread over eight
            scalar/point pairs. Used as the baseline against which crypto
            micro-optimizations are measured. Off by default; opt-in for
            perf work.

endmenu
